.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]cbor::lazy\f[R] reads a message in place, one step at a time, and
copies no byte.
A step by key or index reads the heads up to the child.
.SS cbor::lazy
A position in one top\-level item: the owner of the bytes, the bytes and
an offset.
It holds no decoded value.
Each member function reads the bytes at the offset when it is called.
cbor::lazy::view() and cbor::lazy::take_ownership() set the data
members.
A copy of a lazy is a second position in the same bytes.
Several threads read one lazy at the same time.
A \f[CR]nodelist\f[R] is the result of a path that selects many nodes.
A lazy made with \f[CR]cbor::lazy{}\f[R] holds no top\-level item; each
member function then throws \f[CR]std::logic_error\f[R].
.SS cbor::lazy::take_ownership
Makes the lazy of the top\-level item in \f[CR]encoded\f[R], and moves
the bytes into a new owner.
Nothing is copied.
Every lazy, view and \f[CR]ref\f[R] that comes from the result holds
that owner, so the bytes stay alive as long as one of them lives.
\f[CR]Encoded\f[R] is a container of \f[CR]char\f[R] that owns its
bytes, for example \f[CR]std::string\f[R].
An lvalue, a \f[CR]std::string_view\f[R] and a literal do not compile.
The call reads only the first head, to skip one tag 55799.
A step on the lazy reports broken input.
.SS cbor::lazy::view
Makes the lazy of the top\-level item in the bytes, in place.
Nothing is copied.
The first form reads \f[CR]encoded\f[R], and \f[CR]owner\f[R] keeps
those bytes alive.
The second form reads \f[CR]*owner\f[R].
Every lazy, view and \f[CR]ref\f[R] that comes from the result holds
\f[CR]owner\f[R].
The call does not check that the bytes are one complete item.
A step on the lazy finds a missing part.
No step reads bytes after the item.
The call reads only the first head, to skip one tag 55799.
.SS cbor::lazy::at
Gives the lazy of one child.
\f[CR]at(key)\f[R] gives the value of the first pair of a map whose key
is the text \f[CR]key\f[R].
\f[CR]at(cbor::key{n})\f[R] gives the value of the first pair whose key
is the integer \f[CR]n\f[R]; a bignum key that holds \f[CR]n\f[R]
matches too.
\f[CR]at(index)\f[R] gives the element of an array or of a typed array,
counted from 0.
A text key does not match a byte string with the same bytes.
The call passes a tag that it does not interpret, and reads into a tag
24.
It reads no value; cbor::lazy::get() does.
.SS cbor::lazy::operator[]
Gives the same result as cbor::lazy::at() with the same argument.
It does not throw for a missing key or a wrong index.
The result is a \f[CR]std::expected\f[R], so
\f[CR]l[\(dqa\(dq][\(dqb\(dq]\f[R] does not compile.
A path reads a chain (cbor::lazy::at_path()).
The errors of cbor::lazy::at() apply.
.SS cbor::lazy::at_path
Reads the JSONPath (RFC 9535) \f[CR]Path\f[R] from this lazy as the root
\f[CR]$\f[R].
Without \f[CR]T\f[R] it gives the lazy of the node.
With a scalar \f[CR]T\f[R] it reads the value as cbor::lazy::get() does.
A text, a byte string and a typed array come back as a \f[CR]ref\f[R]
that holds the owner.
\f[CR]nodelist\f[R] gives every selected node.
The form with \f[CR]e\f[R] gives the result type of the query;
\f[CR]e\f[R] comes from \f[CR]cbor::path::compile<T>(path)\f[R], which
parses the path at run time.
A path that is not valid does not compile.
A path that can select more than one node does not compile, except with
\f[CR]nodelist\f[R].
.SS cbor::lazy::get
Reads the value at the lazy as \f[CR]T\f[R].
