.TE
.PP
A \f[CR]ref\f[R] views the bytes and holds their owner.
Nothing is copied.
The call passes a tag that it does not interpret.
.SS cbor::lazy::type
Gives the kind of the value at the lazy.
A tag 2 or 3 whose magnitude fits 64 bits gives
\f[CR]unsigned_integer\f[R] or \f[CR]negative_integer\f[R].
A larger bignum gives \f[CR]unsigned_bignum\f[R] or
\f[CR]negative_bignum\f[R].
A typed array (RFC 8746) gives \f[CR]typed_array\f[R].
Every other tag gives \f[CR]tag\f[R]; the call does not pass it.
.SS cbor::lazy::size
Gives the count of the head: the elements of an array, the pairs of a
map, or the elements of a typed array.
It reads only the head.
It does not read the children.
.SS cbor::lazy::empty
Gives \f[CR]true\f[R] when cbor::lazy::size() gives 0, and
\f[CR]false\f[R] when it gives another count.
The errors of cbor::lazy::size() apply.
.SS cbor::lazy::elements
Gives the elements of an array, or of a typed array, as a view.
Each element of the view is a \f[CR]std::expected<lazy, error>\f[R].
The call reads only the head.
The view reads one element at each step.
.SS cbor::lazy::entries
Gives the pairs of a map, in the order of the bytes, as a view.
Each element of the view is a
\f[CR]std::expected<std::pair<lazy, lazy>, error>\f[R]: the key in
\f[CR]first\f[R], the value in \f[CR]second\f[R].
A key that occurs twice occurs twice in the view.
.SS cbor::lazy::clone
Decodes the whole item at the lazy into a tree of cbor::item, as
cbor::decode() does, except that tags 28 and 29 stay tag nodes, and that
a tag 55799 inside the top\-level item stays a tag node.
The tree holds a copy of each text and byte string.
It does not view the bytes of the lazy, and stays valid after the lazy
and its owner end.
The errors of cbor::decode() apply.
.SS cbor::lazy_elements
The elements of one array, from cbor::lazy::elements().
It is a forward range, a sized range and a borrowed range.
\f[CR]size()\f[R] gives the count of the head.
\f[CR]*it\f[R] gives the lazy of the element, or the error of the
element.
An iterator holds the owner of the bytes, so it stays valid after the
lazy and the view end.
\f[CR]front()\f[R] does not compile.
When the iterator cannot step over an element, \f[CR]*it\f[R] gives
\f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] or another error of the bytes, and each later
position gives that error.
The view gives at most \f[CR]size()\f[R] values.
.SS cbor::lazy_entries
The pairs of one map, from cbor::lazy::entries(), in the order of the
bytes.
It is a forward range, a sized range and a borrowed range.
\f[CR]size()\f[R] gives the count of pairs in the head.
\f[CR]*it\f[R] gives the key in \f[CR]first\f[R] and the value in
\f[CR]second\f[R], or the error of the pair.
An iterator holds the owner of the bytes, so it stays valid after the
lazy and the view end.
\f[CR]front()\f[R] does not compile.
When the iterator cannot step over a key or a value, \f[CR]*it\f[R]
gives \f[CR]too_little_data\f[R] or another error of the bytes, and each
later position gives that error.
The view gives at most \f[CR]size()\f[R] values.
